Imagine you buy a server or a single-board computer like a Raspberry Pi. It doesn't have a monitor, a mouse, or a keyboard attached to it—it sits in a closet or a cloud data center miles away.

Without SSH: You would have to physically drive to the data center, plug in a monitor and a keyboard, and type your commands sitting on the floor.

With SSH: You plug an Ethernet cable into that machine, sit on your comfortable couch, open your laptop's terminal, and run a secure "virtual extension cord" straight to that machine.

When you press Enter on your laptop, the keystrokes travel through that encrypted cord, execute on the remote machine's CPU, and the results stream back onto your screen.
